{0}\\cdot\\boldsymbol{x} = 4\\}$","$\\{\\boldsymbol{x}\\in\\mathbb{R}^3\\colon \\|\\boldsymbol{x}\\| \\lt 4\\}$","$\\mathbb{R}^4$","$\\{\\boldsymbol{x}\\in\\mathbb{R}^3\\colon (1,2,3)\\cdot\\boldsymbol{x} = 4\\}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ac9f8adcb46b767fced8db0f"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_hyperplane_cddf57e8_008","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"In $\\mathbb{R}^2$, what dimension does a hyperplane have?","options":["$2$","$0$","$1$","$3$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"71a06e2dbcbe88a6371feb76"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_hyperplane_cddf57e8_009","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why is the condition $\\boldsymbol{a} \\neq \\boldsymbol{0}$ important in the equation form?","options":["A zero vector makes every equation valid for exactly one point","A zero vector forces the dimension to be $n-1$ for every $c$.","A zero vector gives no normal direction, so it defines no hyperplane","A zero vector is required by the definition of dot product."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"3bbf988b36673f1f8c771a58"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_hyperplane_cddf57e8_010","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which statement about the $xy$-plane is correct?","options":["The $xy$-plane is a $3$-dimensional subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3$.","The $xy$-plane is a $2$-dimensional subspace of the $3$-dimensional space $\\mathbb{R}^3$.","The $xy$-plane is not a subspace because it contains the origin.","The $xy$-plane is a hyperplane only in $\\mathbb{R}^2$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"f968e6c7262d105806fabcd2"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_hyperplane_cddf57e8_011","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"Which of the following is not a hyperplane of $\\mathbb{R}^3$ based on dimension?","options":["The whole space $\\mathbb{R}^3$.","The $xy$-plane.","A two-dimensional subspace of $\\math